  "query_preview": "..\nAn upper trough will move from the southwest and spread rain and \nmixed precipitation late tonight. Precipitation will begin as \nrain in the west overnight and spread east tomorrow morning. \nTemperatures will cool enough by the morning for either a \nrain/snow mix or change to all snow in the west. Higher elevations\nof the Little Rocky Mountains near Zortman have potential for up \nto 3 inches of snow. A Winter Weather Advisory remains in effect \nfrom 11 pm tonight through 5 pm tomorrow afternoon for southwest\nPhilips county including Zortman.\n\nLocations along the Canadian Border west of Scobey could see up \nto an inch of snow. Snow amounts will depend on the timing of the \nrain to mixed precipitation and snow transitions. EC, GFS, and CMC\nensembles continue to have varying higher snowfall amounts \nespecially NW of line from Zortman, Malta to Opheim. \n\nIn addition to wintery weather, a Lake Wind Advisory has been issued\nfor Fort Peck Lake, set to begin 8 AM tomorrow lasting through 5 \nAM Thursday. Breezy conditions northwest winds 15 to 25 mph with \ngusts up to 40 mph are expected.\n\nAs the main upper trough settles into the intermountain west \ntomorrow and Thursday, cooling aloft will drive temperatures \ncloser to normal. Large scale upper troughing continues across the\nwestern US the remainder of the the week. The next system will \nbring another shot of colder air from the north. Some light snow \nis possible but the main impact will be the below normal temps.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Lowered tonight, tomorrow and tomorrow night's temperatures from\n  NBM.\n\n- Overall precipitation and snow amounts have not changed significantly\n  and the Winter Weather advisory remains unchanged.\n\n- Confidence in the timing of precipitation changing to snow is\n  low.",
